Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER
Task name: /root
Sender: /root/alchemy_research
Payload:
Technical verification is complete. The repository and production state are unchanged. The current published Alchemy package supports the requested bindings and routes, but the docs and GitHub `main` branch have a package-name mismatch.

**1. Exact `Cloudflare.Website.Astro` props**

Published `alchemy@2.0.0-beta.70` defines:

```ts
AstroProps<Bindings> extends Omit<
  WorkerProps<Bindings>,
  "vite" | "main" | "assets" | "source" | "script" | "bundle"
>
```

It adds:

```ts
{
  rootDir?: string;
  memo?: MemoOptions;
  sessionKVBindingName?: string | false;
  astro?: {
    site?: string;
    base?: string;
    output?: "server" | "static";
    srcDir?: string;
    publicDir?: string;
    outDir?: string;
    trailingSlash?: "always" | "never" | "ignore";
  };
  assets?: AssetsConfig;
}
```

Because it inherits `WorkerProps`, it also accepts:

```ts
{
  name?: string;
  workersDev?: boolean | WorkersDevConfig;
  env?: Bindings;
  observability?: WorkerObservability;
  domain?: string | WorkerDomainConfig | null;
  routes?: WorkerRouteConfig[];
  compatibility?: { date?: string; flags?: string[] };
}
```

Source:

- [Astro.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Website/Astro.ts)
- [Worker.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers/Worker.ts)

`env` accepts Analytics Engine datasets. The published source includes `Dataset` in `WorkerBindingResource`, so this shape is valid:

```ts
const SiteAnalytics = yield* Cloudflare.AnalyticsEngine.Dataset(
  "SiteAnalytics",
  { dataset: "leenk_site_events" },
);

const ShortlinkAnalytics = yield* Cloudflare.AnalyticsEngine.Dataset(
  "ShortlinkAnalytics",
  { dataset: "leenk_shortlinks_v2" },
);

const StaticFiles = yield* Cloudflare.R2.Bucket("StaticFiles", {
  name: "leenk-static",
});

const Website = yield* Cloudflare.Website.Astro("Website", {
  env: {
    STATIC_FILES: StaticFiles,
    SITE_ANALYTICS: SiteAnalytics,
    SHORTLINK_ANALYTICS: ShortlinkAnalytics,
  },
});
```

Dataset source:

- [AnalyticsEngine/Dataset.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/AnalyticsEngine/Dataset.ts)

**2. Package versions**

Registry metadata on 2026-08-09 reports:

```text
alchemy latest = 2.0.0-beta.70
alchemy next   = 2.0.0-beta.70
latest stable non-prerelease = 0.94.0

@distilled.cloud/astro latest = 0.17.1

effect latest = 3.22.1
effect beta   = 4.0.0-beta.106

@effect/platform-bun beta  = 4.0.0-beta.106
@effect/platform-node beta = 4.0.0-beta.106
```

The current Alchemy package requires Effect 4:

```text
alchemy@2.0.0-beta.70
effect@>=4.0.0-beta.102 || >=4.0.0
@effect/platform-bun@>=4.0.0-beta.102 || >=4.0.0
@effect/platform-node@>=4.0.0-beta.102 || >=4.0.0
```

`@next` is not technically mandatory. It resolves to the same `2.0.0-beta.70` package as `latest`. Pinning exact versions is safer:

```text
alchemy@2.0.0-beta.70
effect@4.0.0-beta.106
@effect/platform-bun@4.0.0-beta.106
@effect/platform-node@4.0.0-beta.106
@distilled.cloud/astro@0.17.1
```

Package metadata:

- [alchemy 2.0.0-beta.70](https://registry.npmjs.org/alchemy/2.0.0-beta.70)
- [@distilled.cloud/astro 0.17.1](https://registry.npmjs.org/@distilled.cloud%2Fastro/0.17.1)

Important mismatch:

- Current docs and published package source use `@distilled.cloud/astro`.
- GitHub `main` still documents `@alchemy.run/cloudflare-frameworks`.

[Current Astro docs](https://alchemy.run/cloudflare/frontend/astro/) · [GitHub source](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Website/Astro.ts)

Pin the published versions and compile-test them before migration.

**3. R2 adoption for `leenk-static`**

The current published R2 provider uses `name`, not `title`:

```ts
const StaticFiles = yield* Cloudflare.R2.Bucket("StaticFiles", {
  name: "leenk-static",
});
```

The provider’s `read` operation queries the exact bucket name. It does not return `Unowned`, so an existing bucket with that exact name is silently recovered into Alchemy state. `--adopt` is not required for this R2 provider.

Do not omit `name`. Without it, Alchemy generates a new physical bucket name.

R2 source:

- [R2/Bucket.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/R2/Bucket.ts)
- [Adopting Resources](https://alchemy.run/cli/adopting-resources/)

Generic `--adopt` behavior still matters for routes and other resources. It is global:

```text
alchemy plan --stage prod
alchemy deploy --stage prod --adopt
```

Run the first plan without `--adopt`. Use it only if the plan reports an ownership collision.

**4. Custom domain and route shapes**

A Website inherits Worker custom-domain support:

```ts
const Website = yield* Cloudflare.Website.Astro("Website", {
  domain: {
    name: "www.example.com",
    aliases: ["example.com"],
    redirects: ["old.example.com"],
  },
  workersDev: false,
});
```

A bare string is shorthand:

```ts
domain: "www.example.com"
```

The zone must already exist. Alchemy creates DNS and the edge certificate.

For path-based routing, `Website.Astro` accepts:

```ts
routes: [
  {
    pattern: "www.example.com/*",
    zoneName: "example.com",
  },
]
```

The standalone route resource requires `zoneId`:

```ts
const Website = yield* Cloudflare.Website.Astro("Website");

yield* Cloudflare.Workers.WorkerRoute("WebsiteRoute", {
  zoneId: zone.zoneId,
  pattern: "www.example.com/*",
  script: Website.workerName,
});
```

`WorkerRoute` has no ownership marker. An existing matching route reports `OwnedBySomeoneElse` unless deployed with `--adopt`.

Sources:

- [Worker.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers/Worker.ts)
- [Route.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers/Route.ts)
- [Custom domains docs](https://alchemy.run/cloudflare/networking/custom-domains/)

Use either `domain` or `routes` for the same hostname. Do not declare both until the plan proves they do not conflict.

**5. Nub invocation**

Nub’s installed CLI confirms:

```text
nub exec <bin> [args...]
```

runs a binary from `node_modules/.bin` without creating another lockfile.

Therefore, after adding Alchemy as a dependency:

```sh
nub exec alchemy plan --stage staging
nub exec alchemy dev
nub exec alchemy deploy --stage prod
```

Package scripts can wrap the same binary:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "alchemy:dev": "alchemy dev",
    "alchemy:plan": "alchemy plan",
    "alchemy:deploy": "alchemy deploy"
  }
}
```

Then use:

```sh
nub run alchemy:plan -- --stage staging
```

Do not use `npm`, `bun`, or `pnpm` for dependency installation in this repository. Alchemy’s docs show Bun commands, but they do not document Nub. Nub’s `exec` behavior is the correct repository-compatible invocation path.

**Changed**

None in the repository or production. Temporary package probes were extracted under `/tmp`.

**Unresolved**

The docs/source package mismatch remains. Use the published package versions above and run a throwaway TypeScript compile before editing the real project.

